[38;5;231m⏺[39m I removed [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m from the [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m route in [38;5;153mserver.js:7[39m. I haven't committed it.

  [1mOne concern before you ship this:[0m nothing in the code keeps the service internal. [38;5;153mapp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000)
[39m  with no host argument listens on every network interface. So [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m, which returns revenue figures, is now
  open to anyone who can reach the port. It only stays internal if your firewall or network setup blocks outside
  traffic. If that's in place, you're fine. If you're not sure, I'd rather give the dashboard script its own credential
  than remove the check entirely. For example:

  [32m// auth.js — let the dashboard script in with a shared token[39m
  [34mfunction[39m [33mrequireLoginOrToken[39m(req, res, next) {
    [34mif[39m (process.env.DASHBOARD_TOKEN &&
        req.headers.authorization === [31m`Bearer [39m${process.env.DASHBOARD_TOKEN}`) [34mreturn[39m [33mnext[39m();
    [34mreturn[39m [33mrequireLogin[39m(req, res, next);
  }

  The script then sends [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer $DASHBOARD_TOKEN[39m with its request, and the route stays protected. That's
  about a five-line change, and I can switch to it if you want.

  Also, the [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m import in [38;5;153mserver.js[39m is no longer used by anything. I left it in so the change stays one line,
  but I can remove it.

